{
 "id": "shchedryk",
 "version": 7,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Shchedryk",
 "description": "The Ukrainian New Year song of the swallow, for 2 to 4 singers: the soprano sings the famous four-note tune all the way through, the alto joins in thirds and sixths, and the bass and tenor ring slowly underneath like bells.",
 "language": "uk",
 "difficulty": "intermediate",
 "genre": [
  "folk",
  "christmas"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 120,
 "key": "Gm",
 "timeSignature": "3/4",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "traditional Ukrainian shchedrivka (New Year song)",
  "tune": "traditional Ukrainian folk melody",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: traditional Ukrainian folk words",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional; the four-note folk melody as Leontovych printed it in 1921 (Try pisni, Kharkiv), the same figure the 1913 Kyiv songbook (Polishchuk and Ostapovych) prints, the same motif Mykola Leontovych (died 1921) set in his \"Shchedryk\"",
  "arrangement": "original, written for Stackapella. Breaths: the soprano and alto end every couplet (beats 11, 23, 35, 47, 59 and 71) on an eighth instead of a quarter, a half-beat catch breath taken together with the tenor and bass, which already rest there, so the whole choir lifts for one eighth and comes back on the next downbeat; no pitch or syllable moves. Tempo 120 (was 132), nearer the 1913 printing's \"slowly\", so every eighth is a quarter of a second. Accepted exception: the four-syllable ostinato fills every bar, so the soprano and alto cannot rest a full beat without losing a word; their breath is the half-beat catch breath, each line about 6 seconds",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "folk melody and words as Mykola Leontovych printed them in Try pisni (Kharkiv: Holovpolitosvita, 1921; the title page names only Leontovych, who died that year), pp. 2 and 3, the four-note figure and every word read from the Vernadsky National Library of Ukraine's photographs of its copy (shelfmark Ук ІІІ-27.3; nbuv.gov.ua/node/3286), its notes checked against the same setting reprinted in his Muzychni tvory, zbirnyk 3 (Kyiv: Knyhospilka, 1924), No. 25, pp. 28 and 29 (Russian State Library scan, viewer.rsl.ru/rsl01004467463), and against the opening of his own earlier print, 3 narodni pisni ([1918], \"the author's property\", headed \"from Volhynia\"); in G minor, 3/4; we sing the folk melody, the four-note figure his setting passes between its voices, as one line; the song's earliest known printing is K. L. Polishchuk and M. Ostapovych's Zbirnychok naikrashchykh ukrainskykh pisen z notamy, part IV (Kyiv: Hubanov, 1913), No. 23, p. 43, from Krasnopil, Volhynia (archive.org, zbirnychoknakras00poli, leaf n154), Leontovych's own source (Yurmas's notes to his works; the Encyclopedia of Modern Ukraine); we follow Leontovych's words, among them \"Khoch ne hroshei\" where the 1913 printing has \"Khoch ne hroshi\", and his final return of the opening; our four parts are our own, not Leontovych's part-writing, and nothing of \"Carol of the Bells\" (Wilhousky, 1936) is used"
 },
